We have \begin{align*}
f(f(x))&=\dfrac{a\left(\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}\right) +b}{\left(c\left(\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}\right)\right)+d}\\
&=\dfrac{a(ax+b)+b(cx+d)}{cx+d(ax+b)}
\end{align*}

This looks rather challenging in terms of simplification. But notice that generating the same fraction in the denominator ($cx+d$) implies that $\dfrac{ax+b}{cx+d}=x$, so: \begin{align*}a(ax+b)+b(cx+d)&=a^2x+ab+bcx+bd\\&=(a^2+bc)x+(ab+bd)\end{align*} On the other hand, \begin{align*}cx+d(ax+b)&=(ac+bd) x+bd \end{align*} Putting these into the form of a two fractions: $\dfrac{(a^2+bc)x+(ab+bd)}{(ac+bd)x+bd}$

The only time $f(f(x))$ can equal $x$ for all $x$ except $-\frac{d}{c}$ is when the denominator and numerator cancel out. This is possible when $bd = 0$ and $ac +bd +ab +bc = 0$. Since $abcd \neq 0$, $c$ and $d$, $a$ and $b$, as well as $b$ and $d$ cannot both equal 0. Therefore, $ ac+bd = 0 \rightarrow c = 0 \rightarrow a=d \rightarrow a+d=0$
