Since the tangent function has period $180^\circ$, we can find an equivalent angle between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$ by subtracting multiples of $360^\circ$ (which is $2 \cdot 180^\circ$) from $3825^\circ$.  That is, $\tan 3825^\circ = \tan (3825 - 2 \cdot 180)^\circ = \tan 135^\circ$.  The angle $135^\circ$ is in the second quadrant; halfway between $135^\circ$ and $180^\circ$ is $150^\circ$, which divides the angle into two $30^\circ$ angles.  By the Basic Tangent Identity, this tells us $\tan 150^\circ = -\tan 30^\circ = -\frac{1}{\sqrt 3}$.  This is negative since $150^\circ$ is in the second quadrant.

Now, we use the tangent angle addition formula to find $\tan 135^\circ = \tan (150 - 15)^\circ$.  Using the formula:  \begin{align*}
\tan 135^\circ &= \tan (150 - 15)^\circ\\
&= \frac{\tan 150^\circ - \tan 15^\circ}{1 + \tan 150^\circ \tan 15^\circ}  = \frac{-\frac{1}{\sqrt 3} - \frac{1 - \sqrt 3}{2}}{1 + \left(-\frac{1}{\sqrt 3}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{1 - \sqrt 3}{2}\right)} \\
&= \frac{-2 - \sqrt 3 + 3}{-2\sqrt 3 + 1 + \sqrt 3}\\
&= \frac{1 - \sqrt 3}{-1 - \sqrt 3}
\end{align*}  We can rationalize the denominator:  \begin{align*}
\tan 135^\circ &= \frac{1 - \sqrt 3}{-1 - \sqrt 3} \cdot \frac{-1 + \sqrt 3}{-1 + \sqrt 3}\\
&= \frac{1 - \sqrt 3}{4 + 2\sqrt 3 - 2\sqrt 3 - 3}\\
&= \boxed{-\frac{1}{2}}.
\end{align*}
