The diagonal of the cube then divides the sphere into 6 congruent spherical segments. One of these segments has a height equal to a radius of the sphere, and is composed of a spherical sector, a cone, and a spherical segment of the sphere (the one of which we are trying to find the volume of). Therefore, by Double the Cone Formula $$V_{sphere} = 6\cdot\frac13r^2\theta = 2r^3\theta$$ where $r$ and $\theta$ are the radius and dihedral angle of the spherical segment, respectively.  What remains is the volume of the spherical segment. Using the cylinder definition of the height, we construct a cylinder with height $h$, base radius $r$, and ${60^\circ}$ angle curvature, thus $h=r$. It follows that the low half of the cylinder is $(60^\circ/360^\circ)\pi r^2$, while the rest of the cylinder is $$\pi r^2\frac{6r}{5}= \pi r^3\frac65.$$ Using the Double Cone formula backwards for the cylinder, the volume taken up by the spherical segment is $$\frac5{5\pi/3}\pi r^3\frac65 - \frac32 r^2 h = 2r^3 - \frac32r^3= \frac12r^3. $$ Therefore, our ratio is $$\frac{\frac12r^3}{2r^3} = \frac{r^3}{4r^3}=\frac{1}{4}.\qquad\blacksquare$$
