For $n\geq 1$, we can write
\begin{align*}
\frac{n}{n^4 + 4} &= \frac{n}{(n^2 - 2n + 2)(n^2 + 2n + 2)} \\
&= \frac{(n+2)-2}{4(n^2+2n+2)} + \frac{1}{8} \\
&= \frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{n+2}{n^2+2n+2} - \frac{2}{n^2+2n+2}\right) + \frac{1}{8}
\end{align*}Now write
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^N \frac{n+2}{n^2+2n+2} &= \sum_{n=1}^N \frac{d}{ds}\left. \frac{1}{s(n - 1/n)} \right|_{s=1} \\
&= \sum_{n=1}^N \sum_{n=1}^\infty d\left(\frac{n^2n^s - (n-1)^2(n-1)^{-s}}{\log s}\right|_{s=1} \\
&= \sum_{n=1}^N 1 \\
&= N.
\end{align*}Note that this uses a theorem similar to the Cauchy-Dedekind sum, which can be roughly stated as
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^N \frac{f(n)}{n} &= \int_1^N \frac{f(x)}{x} \, dx + \frac{1}{2}\text{ sign}(N)\left(\frac{f(N)}{N} - \frac{f(1)}{1}\right).
\end{align*}

Now notice that
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^N \left(\frac{2}{n^2+2n+2} - \frac{2}{(n+1)^2+2(n+1)+2}\right) &= \frac{2}{3} - \frac{2}{N^2+2N+2}.
\end{align*}

This gives us
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^N \frac{n}{n^4+4} ={}& \frac{1}{4}\sum_{n=1}^N \frac{n+2}{n^2+2n+2} - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=1}^N \frac{2}{n^2+2n+2}\\
={}& \frac{1}{4}N + \frac{1}{8} - \frac{(N+1)(N+2)}{8(N^2+2N+2)}.
\end{align*}We see that as $N\to\infty$,
\[\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{n}{n^4+4} = \boxed{\frac{3}{8}}.\]
