label("P",(4,4),N);
label("A",(0,4),N);
draw((4,0)--(0,4)--(4,4)--(8,4)--(4,0));
[/asy] \textbf{Case 2:} Now we have another two pairs perpendicular at $P$ and $Q$, on $BCQ$ and $ADQ$.  These give $\binom{2}{2}\cdot2=2$ more triangles.  Lastly, $\binom{1}{2}=0$ triangles can be formed with points $R,Q$, and $D$, where $R$ is the midpoint of $AD$.  Similarly $\binom{1}{2}=0$ triangles can be formed with points $R,Q$, and $P$. Thus there are $2+0+0=\boxed{2}$ triangles in total, in this case.

[asy]
