draw((4,0)--(4,4));
label("D",(0,0),S);
label("Q",(4,0),S);
label("C",(8,0),S);
label("B",(8,4),N);
label("P",(4,4),N);
label("A",(0,4),N);
draw((0,4)--(8,0)--(4,4)--(0,4));
[/asy] \textbf{Case 3:} And finally, we have $\binom{2}{2}\cdot2=2$ triangles on $BD,DCQ$ and $PCQ$.  Similarly $\binom{1}{2}=0$ triangles can be formed with $G,M$, and $P$ where $G$ is the intersection of segment $DB$ and the perpendicular to segment $AD$ through $B$, and $M$ is the midpoint of segment $BC$. (No figure included.) Thus there are $\boxed{2+0=\boxed{2}}$ triangles, in this case.

